---
/**
 * Our work — categorized. The section Overclock hands over to.
 *
 * Nothing on this screen moves. That is the whole of the layout: one sticky
 * screen holding every category stacked on top of one another, and the scroll
 * dissolves them away one at a time — the same dissolve, from the bottom edge
 * upwards behind a wide feather, that brings the reader into this section out
 * of Overclock. The words are not in that dissolve; they have a fade of their
 * own that runs earlier and faster, so no two categories are ever readable at
 * once. An earlier cut had the copy travelling —
 * each block carried up and off the top of the window by the scroll while a
 * sticky ground stayed put — and this replaces it. Two layers moving at
 * different rates is a parallax; one layer changing over is a slide, and a
 * slide is what a numbered list of services wants to be.
 *
 * Three things per category, in one frame:
 *
 *   the number    01, small, at the top of the copy column.
 *   the copy      headline, pitch and button, at the foot of the left column,
 *                 on the same gutter Overclock's copy sits on — the two
 *                 sections are adjacent and their columns have to line up.
 *   the artwork   the category's shot, full bleed. It IS the ground — the
 *                 section's black is only what is under it while it loads, and
 *                 what shows through the veil.
 *
 *                 It was a panel on the right for a day, taken from the
 *                 reference, where the shot is a render on transparency with
 *                 the black of the page around it. Given a real photograph
 *                 that arrangement is a rectangle sitting on a screen with a
 *                 visible edge down the middle of it, which is not what the
 *                 reference looks like at all.
 *
 *   the veil      a dark wash over that shot, heaviest on the left and gone by
 *                 the right. White type needs a ground, and a photograph is
 *                 not one — this is what keeps the column legible whatever the
 *                 category's picture is doing behind it.
 *
 * The scroll length is `--cat-step` per category and nothing else — no travel
 * to buy room for. Five of them is about four and a half screens, roughly half
 * what the travelling cut cost.
 *
 * Black, and it declares itself so with `data-nav-over="dark"`: the nav's
 * capsule turns to glass over it. The join above it is a black curtain that
 * rises from the foot of the window and fades — see initWorkHandover in
 * src/scripts/workCategories.ts and `[data-handover]` in index.astro.
 *
 * `--work-outro` is still shared with Two ways in, which is pulled up over this
 * section's last held frame by the same number. Changing it here alone tears
 * that join.
 *
 * One layout at every width above the phone breakpoint, and it stands up with
 * the stylesheet alone: without JS the first category is the section and the
 * rest are simply not painted. The crossfades are the only thing the script
 * does here.
 */

interface Category {
  /** Rendered as-is — the frame sets these zero-padded. */
  number: string;
  label: string;
  /** One line about the category, under the headline. */
  blurb: string;
  /** Where "See work" goes. */
  href: string;
  /**
   * The flat panel this category's artwork box is painted in while its shot is
   * still being chosen — a placeholder with the footprint of the real thing,
   * so the frame is never simply empty on the right.
   *
   * Near-black on purpose, and stepped so no two neighbours are the same
   * value: the crossfade between two categories is the only thing that
   * announces the change, and on five identical panels the section would read
   * as one screen that never moves.
   */
  tone: string;
  /**
   * The shot itself, without an extension — rendered as a <picture> with an
   * avif source and a webp fallback, so '/media/work-website-design' wants
   * work-website-design.avif and work-website-design.webp beside it.
   *
   * Optional, and most of them are still to come: without it the category is
   * its flat `tone`. Named per category rather than derived, because which
   * shot stands for a whole category is a choice — swapping one is this line
   * and nothing else.
   */
  art?: string;
  /**
   * `object-position` for that shot, for the ones whose crop needs steering.
   * The box bleeds off the right and the bottom, so the default holds the
   * artwork's top-left corner — which is the corner the reference keeps whole.
   */
  focus?: string;
}

interface Props {
  categories?: Category[];
  viewLabel?: string;
}

const {
  viewLabel = 'See work',
  categories = [
    {
      number: '01',
      label: 'Website Design & Development',
      blurb:
        'Marketing sites and platforms, designed and built end to end — so what ships is what was drawn, at the speed the launch actually needs.',
      href: '/work',
      art: '/media/work-website-design',
      tone: '#101010',
    },
    {
      number: '02',
      label: 'Product Design',
      blurb:
        'The screens people use every day: flows, states, and the systems underneath them, drawn so the product stays coherent as it grows.',
      href: '/work',
      art: '/media/work-product-design',
      tone: '#151515',
    },
    {
      number: '03',
      label: 'Brand Design',
      blurb:
        'Marks, type, colour and motion, with the rules that keep them holding together everywhere the brand turns up.',
      href: '/work',
      art: '/media/work-brand-design',
      tone: '#101010',
    },
    {
      number: '04',
      label: 'Pitch Deck',
      blurb:
        'The story a room has to follow in twenty minutes — cut, structured and designed so the argument carries without you narrating it.',
      href: '/work',
      art: '/media/work-pitch-deck',
      tone: '#151515',
    },
    {
      number: '05',
      label: 'Marketing Design',
      blurb:
        'Campaign and social work that keeps the brand recognisable at the size a feed actually shows it, in the volume a calendar actually needs.',
      href: '/work',
      /* No `art` yet — this shot is still to come, so the category paints its
         flat tone until it does. */
      tone: '#101010',
    },
  ],
} = Astro.props;
---

<section
  class="cat"
  id="work"
  aria-label="Our work"
  data-nav-section="Our work"
  data-nav-over="dark"
  data-cat
>
  {/*
    The screen. Stuck to the top of the window for as long as the section is
    passing, with every category stacked inside it.

    `position: sticky`, not a pinned ScrollTrigger: sticky is the browser
    holding an element still inside a scroll it is not otherwise touching,
    which is exactly what is wanted and is why nothing in this section takes
    the scrollbar away from the reader. It also means the layout stands up
    with the stylesheet alone.
  */}
  <div class="cat__stick" data-cat-dissolve>
    {
      categories.map((category, index) => (
        /* Reverse paint order — the FIRST category is the topmost layer.
           Handing over is a matter of taking the one on top away, so the one
           underneath has to already be there, whole, waiting. Document order
           would stack them the other way and there would be nothing to
           uncover. */
        <div class="cat__screen" data-cat-screen style={`z-index:${categories.length - index}`}>
          {/* Picture and veil as one thing, because the mask that takes this
              category away has to take both — a photograph dissolving out from
              under its own wash would leave the wash behind as a grey sheet. */}
          <div class="cat__pic" data-cat-pic>
          {/* The ground. Painted in `tone` underneath so the screen is never
              bare while the file is still loading — or before there is one. */}
          <div class="cat__art" style={`background:${category.tone}`} aria-hidden="true">
            {category.art && (
              <picture>
                <source srcset={`${category.art}.avif`} type="image/avif" />
                <img
                  src={`${category.art}.webp`}
                  alt=""
                  decoding="async"
                  {/* The first category's shot is what the reader is dissolved
                      into out of Overclock — it has to be decoded before that
                      join starts, not when it scrolls into view, because by
                      then it is already on screen and fading up. The rest are
                      four screens further down. */}
                  loading={index === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
                  fetchpriority={index === 0 ? 'high' : undefined}
                  style={category.focus ? `object-position:${category.focus}` : undefined}
                />
              </picture>
            )}
          </div>

          {/* Over the picture, under the words. One per screen rather than one
              for the section: it belongs to this category's photograph and
              leaves with it. */}
          <div class="cat__veil" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          </div>

          <div class="cat__group" data-cat-copy>
            <span class="cat__num">{category.number}</span>

            <h3 class="cat__headline">{category.label}</h3>

            <p class="cat__blurb">{category.blurb}</p>

            <a class="cat__cta" href={category.href} data-magnetic="0.2">
              <span class="cat__cta-fill" aria-hidden="true" />
              <span class="cat__cta-label" data-magnetic-inner>
                {viewLabel}
              </span>
              <span class="u-sr-only">{` — ${category.label}`}</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  {/*
    The scroll length, and nothing else — one step per category, empty. The
    screens above are stacked and stationary, so there is no block whose own
    height could measure the section: these are what the crossfades are
    triggered against, and what makes the sticky screen stay.
  */}
  <ul class="cat__track" aria-hidden="true">
    {categories.map(() => <li class="cat__step" data-cat-panel />)}
  </ul>
</section>

<style>
  .cat {
    /* What one category costs in scroll. There is no travel to pay for any
       more — the whole of it is the crossfade and the beat after it — so this
       is shorter than the travelling cut's 155svh by a long way.

       Turn this one number to change the pace. */
    --cat-step: 90svh;

    /* The copy's inset. Deliberately the shared section padding rather than a
       column inset of its own: Overclock is directly above this and its copy
       sits on that gutter, so anything else would step the two sections' left
       edges out of line across the join. */
    --cat-inset-x: var(--section-padding-x);


    /* How far this section is pulled up underneath Overclock's last screen.

       The join between the two is a dissolve, and a dissolve needs both
       states in the same place at the same time: Overclock stays pinned and
       is masked away from its bottom edge, and what has to be behind it as
       that happens is this section, already parked at its sticky position and
       not moving. Overlapping them is what buys that.

       Spent twice, and it has to be. Overclock's pin grows by one of these so
       the chapter is held still through the join; that growth pushes
       everything below it down by the same distance, so the first is only
       cancelling the push and the second is what actually produces the
       overlap. With one, measured, this section's top still arrived at the
       window's foot exactly as the join ended and slid up through the whole
       of it — the join had nothing behind it but the page.

       src/scripts/overclock.ts does not read this number. It measures the
       distance between this section's top and its track's, which is the same
       distance by construction and cannot be got wrong by counting. */
    --cat-join: 100svh;

    position: relative;
    /* Under Overclock for the length of the join — see `--cat-join` above and
       the paint-order note in index.astro, which holds the other half of it. */
    z-index: 1;
    margin-top: calc(-2 * var(--cat-join));
    /* The layers sort themselves out inside the section and nowhere else.

       Deliberately not `overflow: clip`. Any overflow other than `visible` on
       an ancestor makes that ancestor the scrollport a sticky child sticks
       inside — and this one does not scroll, so the screen would simply sit at
       the top of the section and leave with it. */
    isolation: isolate;
    background: var(--color-bg-work);
    color: var(--color-ink-invert);
    padding-bottom: var(--work-outro);
  }

  /* --- The sticky screen ---------------------------------------------------- */
  .cat__stick {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    height: 100svh;
    /* The artwork bleeds past the right and bottom of this box on purpose. */
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .cat__screen {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  /* --- The picture layer ----------------------------------------------------
     What the handover takes away. Every category's picture is opaque and
     stacked, first on top, so all a change has to do is dissolve the top one
     and the next is already underneath at full strength.

     The mask is the same instrument, and the same shape, as the one that
     brings the reader into this section out of Overclock — see the join note
     in index.astro. A feather wider than half the screen, so what travels is
     never an edge.

     Only while a picture is actually going: `data-cat-wiping` is put on by the
     script for the length of that category's handover. Five permanently masked
     full-screen layers would be five compositing surfaces for the sake of one
     that is moving. */
  .cat__pic {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  [data-cat-pic][data-cat-wiping] {
    --cat-wipe: 0;
    --cat-feather: 55%;
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(
      to top,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0) calc(var(--cat-wipe) * 1% - var(--cat-feather)),
      rgb(0 0 0 / 1) calc(var(--cat-wipe) * 1%)
    );
    mask-image: linear-gradient(
      to top,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0) calc(var(--cat-wipe) * 1% - var(--cat-feather)),
      rgb(0 0 0 / 1) calc(var(--cat-wipe) * 1%)
    );
  }

  /* The words do not dissolve with the picture they sit on, and that is the
     point of separating them. A masked headline comes apart letter by letter
     from its baseline upwards, and worse, it is still legible over the words
     of the category arriving underneath. They fade instead, on their own
     clock, and a good deal earlier — gone before the next set has begun.

     Only the first category's copy is on to start with: without the script
     that is the one category the section shows, which is the same thing it
     showed before. */
  .cat__screen:not(:first-child) .cat__group {
    opacity: 0;
    /* Paired with opacity, and kept in step by GSAP's autoAlpha: four
       transparent copy columns are still four composited surfaces on every
       scrolled frame, four hidden ones are not. */
    visibility: hidden;
  }

  /* --- The artwork ---------------------------------------------------------- */
  .cat__art {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  /* Angled rather than straight across, and gone entirely by the right-hand
     edge: the copy is a column on the left, so that is the only side that has
     to be protected, and a wash over the whole frame would just be a grey
     photograph. The steps in the middle are what keep the ramp from reading as
     a band — a two-stop gradient across a whole screen has a visible middle. */
  .cat__veil {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(
      100deg,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0.88) 0%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0.72) 22%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0.38) 52%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0.08) 78%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0) 100%
    );
  }

  /* Pinned to the box rather than sized by percentage inside it. `height: 100%`
     on a <picture> only resolves if every box between it and the panel has a
     height of its own, which is one refactor away from failing silently — and
     when it fails the <img> falls back to its intrinsic size and overflows the
     section instead of filling its corner. */
  .cat__art picture,
  .cat__art img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    /* Centred, now that the box is the whole screen: whatever a shot has to
       give up to fit a window it should give up evenly, off both sides or off
       the top and bottom. A category whose crop needs steering says so with
       `focus`. */
    object-position: center;
  }

  /* --- The copy -------------------------------------------------------------
     At the foot of the left column, on the section's own gutter. Bottom
     padding rather than centring: the reference sits the block low, and the
     same measurement dresses Overclock's copy directly above it. */
  .cat__group {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    gap: clamp(0.875rem, 1.6vh, 1.25rem);
    width: 100%;
    max-width: var(--content-max-width);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: var(--cat-inset-x);
    padding-bottom: clamp(3rem, 9vh, 6.5rem);
    /* The artwork is decoration and the copy is not, but this box covers the
       whole screen so that its own children can be placed in it. Without this
       it would swallow every pointer event over the artwork. */
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .cat__group > * {
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  .cat__num {
    margin-bottom: clamp(0.5rem, 1.4vh, 1rem);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.5);
  }

  .cat__headline {
    /* 3.9vw is the hero title's own ramp — the two are the largest type on the
       page and should be the same size at the same window. */
    max-width: clamp(18rem, 33vw, 29.5rem);
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 3.9vw, 3.75rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.14;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  .cat__blurb {
    max-width: clamp(18rem, 33vw, 29.5rem);
    font-size: clamp(0.9375rem, 1.11vw, 1rem);
    line-height: 1.625;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.62);
  }

  /* Inverted from the light cut of this section: a black pill on a black
     ground is not a button. Same proportions, opposite ink. */
  .cat__cta {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-height: 2.75rem;
    margin-top: clamp(0.75rem, 2vh, 1.5rem);
    padding: 0.6875rem 1.625rem;
    border-radius: 0.625rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--color-bg-primary);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    will-change: transform;
  }

  .cat__cta-fill {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: var(--color-ink);
    transform: scaleY(0);
    transform-origin: bottom center;
    transition: transform 0.55s var(--ease-out-expo);
  }

  .cat__cta:hover .cat__cta-fill,
  .cat__cta:focus-visible .cat__cta-fill {
    transform: scaleY(1);
  }

  .cat__cta-label {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    transition: color 0.4s var(--ease-out-expo);
  }

  .cat__cta:hover .cat__cta-label,
  .cat__cta:focus-visible .cat__cta-label {
    color: var(--color-ink-invert);
  }

  /* --- The track ------------------------------------------------------------
     Pulled up over the sticky screen so the first step starts at the section's
     own top: the screen is in flow and is a window tall, and without this the
     list would begin a window below where the reader sees the section start.
     Zero-ink content — these are measurements, not elements. */
  .cat__track {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 0;
    /* Two things at once. The window cancels the sticky screen's own height,
       so the first step begins where the reader sees the section begin rather
       than a window below it. The join is then added back, because the first
       window of this section is spent being dissolved into — the screen is
       there, still, fading up under Overclock — and a category that started
       counting during it would already be handing over to the next one by the
       time the reader could see either. */
    margin-top: calc(-100svh + var(--cat-join));
  }

  .cat__step {
    height: var(--cat-step);
  }

  /* --- Responsive ----------------------------------------------------------- */
  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .cat {
      --cat-step: 70svh;
    }

    /* Straight down rather than across. A phone column is the whole width, so
       there is no clear side for an angled wash to fade towards — the copy
       sits at the foot of the screen and the protection has to be under it. */
    .cat__veil {
      background: linear-gradient(
        to top,
        rgb(0 0 0 / 0.9) 0%,
        rgb(0 0 0 / 0.72) 34%,
        rgb(0 0 0 / 0.4) 62%,
        rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2) 100%
      );
    }

    .cat__headline,
    .cat__blurb {
      max-width: none;
    }

    .cat__headline {
      font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 8vw, 2.5rem);
    }
  }
</style>
